WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.640
Peter Parker spends his time in "No Way&nbsp;
Home" trying to send the multiverse's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.760
villains back home – but what happens in&nbsp;
their worlds once they get there? And wait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:15.920
when exactly are they coming from? These&nbsp;
confusing moments, and more, explained next.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:21.200
"No Way Home" opens at exactly the point where&nbsp;
"Far From Home" ended. A breaking news report&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.520
from the Daily Bugle's incorrigible J.&nbsp;
Jonah Jameson reveals two key pieces of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.080
information to the world: Spider-Man is&nbsp;
Peter Parker, and he killed Mysterio.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:33.600
It's no surprise, that Mysterio was&nbsp;
able to capture footage of Peter to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.120
be released after his death, but&nbsp;
how exactly did the villain manage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.000
to convince the world that Spider-Man had&nbsp;
murdered him and unleashed a drone army?

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.880
Right from the start, there were some major flaws&nbsp;
in Mysterio's posthumous plan. For one thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:50.800
anyone in London during Mysterio's attack&nbsp;
would've been able to see that Spider-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.560
was battling all the drones that attacked&nbsp;
the city. Even though Mysterio managed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.800
fake Peter's voice ordering the drones&nbsp;
to strike, it's hard to believe that not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.280
a single camera in London captured some&nbsp;
footage of the webslinger's good deeds.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:08.880
Because all Mysterio's framing plot could rely on&nbsp;
was some faked audio and footage of himself merely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.560
saying that Spider-Man was about to attack&nbsp;
him, it all fell apart pretty quickly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:17.200
Peter crawled his way out of any legal troubles&nbsp;
with ease, but there's always someone who will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.760
fall for a conspiracy theory. Most of the world&nbsp;
might be convinced of Spider-Man's innocence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:26.720
but Jameson and the Bugle's fans are still holding&nbsp;
out for solid proof of Spider-Man's misdeeds.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:32.880
Peter being outed as Spider-Man causes problems&nbsp;
for just about everyone in his life. To him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.080
it suddenly looks like the future&nbsp;
he envisioned for himself — happily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:40.240
studying at college alongside his best friend&nbsp;
and girlfriend — will never become a reality.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.680
On top of that, he and Aunt May have to&nbsp;
shelter in Happy's apartment to hide from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:47.760
any Spider-Man conspiracy theorists still&nbsp;
out there. Reeling from all the chaos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:52.640
Peter thinks of the one person who might be&nbsp;
able to magically make his troubles go away.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:55.680
When Peter shows up at the Sanctum&nbsp;
Santorum, however, Doctor Strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.160
informs him that he's no longer the Sorcerer&nbsp;
Supreme. Because the role is so important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:04.800
it needed to be filled immediately after&nbsp;
Thanos' Snap turned Strange to dust in the wind.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.440
The only person who could properly fill Doctor&nbsp;
Strange's shoes was, of course, Wong, who in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:14.000
reality has been working with the magical arts&nbsp;
for longer than Strange himself. While we might've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.960
expected Strange to take his position back when&nbsp;
he un-Blipped, that would hardly be fair to Wong.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:24.480
Besides, Strange seems perfectly content to focus&nbsp;
on his own pursuits, and Wong must be at least a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:29.760
little relieved to have some say in what hijinks&nbsp;
Strange gets up to — not that Wong's influence was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.520
able to prevent Strange from breaking open&nbsp;
the multiverse while trying to help Peter.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.680
The first villain to burst onto the scene&nbsp;
is none other than Doc Ock. When he arrives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:45.600
he's clearly going for mass destruction, and he&nbsp;
starts off poised to give the MCU's Spider-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.640
just as much of a run for his money as he did&nbsp;
in Sam Raimi's "Spider-Man 2" back in 2004.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.440
"Hi. Do we – do I know you?"

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.640
Luckily, today's Peter has access to some&nbsp;
tech that Doc Ock's previous foe did not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.000
When a giant metal tentacle&nbsp;
tears into Peter's Stark suit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.480
the severed nanobots fuse into Ock's arms&nbsp;
and hand over their control to Peter.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:12.000
It's great that Peter finally has the upper hand&nbsp;
in his fight, but how were those nanobots able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.120
interface with Doc Ock's technology? We have to&nbsp;
hand this one to Tony Stark, who's always able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:22.800
to think one or 10 steps ahead of any problem&nbsp;
he might encounter. This isn't the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:28.080
Stark's tech has been shown to be adaptable and&nbsp;
capable of more or less thinking for itself, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:33.680
clearly, Peter's nanobot-powered suit was designed&nbsp;
to change depending on the problems he encounters.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.320
"No Way Home" gives another look at the nanobots'&nbsp;
pseudo-intelligence later in the film when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:43.840
slither off a pacified Doc Ock's arms and reform&nbsp;
into a cool looking spider logo on Peter's suit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.880
Without a doubt, Peter owes this lucky&nbsp;
break to Tony, and now that EDITH is gone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:52.880
this might've been the last time his mentor's&nbsp;
technology is able to get him out of a jam.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:58.480
Anyone who didn't spend the months leading&nbsp;
up to "No Way Home" on an internet sabbatical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:02.400
knew that Jamie Foxx was returning to&nbsp;
play Electro. After trailers and stills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.640
showed a very different-looking villain than&nbsp;
the one featured in "The Amazing Spider-Man 2,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:11.280
everyone began wondering what universe&nbsp;
this Electro really belonged to.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.200
However, when Electro first arrives in the&nbsp;
film, he looks like his old blue self for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.640
just a flash before yellow&nbsp;
electricity floods his body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:22.960
and he changes back into a more Jamie&nbsp;
Foxx-looking version of the character.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:27.040
"I'm happy we got a new, new, a&nbsp;
brand new start. A brand new look."

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:32.000
There are a few different things going on with&nbsp;
Electro's transformation. Outside the world of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.960
the movies, Electro's design in "The Amazing&nbsp;
Spider-Man 2" wasn't particularly popular, so a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:41.680
reskin was never going to be out of the question.&nbsp;
"No Way Home" could've left the change up to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:47.120
magic of the multiverse, but the film actually&nbsp;
takes a bigger step towards a genuine explanation.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:51.680
Since he first gained his powers, Electro's&nbsp;
body has been entirely composed of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:57.680
electrical energy in his world. He says that&nbsp;
the electricity in the MCU feels different — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:02.240
more powerful — than that of his home universe.&nbsp;
It doesn't take a scientist to imagine that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.960
drawing on a different form of energy to create&nbsp;
his body could give Electro a different look.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:14.960
So ... how are all these bad guys alive? This&nbsp;
was one of the biggest questions leading up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:19.840
to the release of "No Way Home," and the film&nbsp;
doesn't come right out with a solid answer for it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:24.720
Each of Spidey's multiversal villains seem to&nbsp;
have been pulled into the MCU from a point very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:30.480
near their death in their own universes. Doc Ock&nbsp;
and Sandman remember the death of Norman Osborn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:35.600
and Electro can recall the Lizard's story, but&nbsp;
none of the villains are aware of their own fate.

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:40.320
Electro gives the clearest answer, as he&nbsp;
states that the last thing he can remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:45.520
is Spider-Man using an overloaded power grid&nbsp;
against him in battle. From that, it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:50.080
safe to assume that all the villains were pulled&nbsp;
from the third acts of their respective stories.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:54.880
The presence of some other important figures&nbsp;
from previous universes raises even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.200
questions. Both multiversal Spider-Men&nbsp;
explicitly arrive from some point in time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.960
after they were last seen on the big screen.&nbsp;
Obviously, that's great for the audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:05.440
and it gives us a deeper sense&nbsp;
of closure with the characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:09.600
but "No Way Home" doesn't explain why&nbsp;
there's such a discrepancy between the heroes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:14.160
and the villains. This one will have to be&nbsp;
chalked up to the magic of the multiverse.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:20.480
One of the best parts about "No Way Home"&nbsp;
is getting to see Peter's friends truly join&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:25.280
in on all the action. Ned and MJ play key&nbsp;
roles in the film, and without their help,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:30.000
there's no way that Peter would've survived&nbsp;
to see a brand new day. Ned has been Peter's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.320
"man in the chair" since "Homecoming," but&nbsp;
he takes a big step up in this adventure.

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:38.800
When Ned and MJ first arrive at the Sanctum&nbsp;
Santorum, he mentions a rumor in his family that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:44.320
some of their ancestors had magical abilities.&nbsp;
Later, Ned ends up with Doctor Strange's sling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:49.040
ring, and sure enough, he's able to use it&nbsp;
to open multiple portals throughout the film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:54.720
Ned is astounded by his new abilities, but&nbsp;
his grandmother — who presumably could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:59.040
some deeper insight into the family's&nbsp;
magical past — hardly blinks an eye.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:03.520
We already know that magical abilities aren't&nbsp;
necessarily linked to lineage. Unless it's later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:07.840
revealed that Doctor Strange has some magic&nbsp;
in his family's past, he became a sorcerer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:12.720
through nothing more mysterious than rigorous&nbsp;
study and hard work. It's possible that some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:17.360
latent ability in Ned's genetics allowed him to&nbsp;
focus some energy through a sling ring with ease,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:21.680
but without further instruction, it's&nbsp;
unlikely that he'll become a true sorcerer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:26.320
Of course, we have to wonder, does&nbsp;
Ned even remember using magic now?

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:31.520
When Spider-Man villains from alternate&nbsp;
universes start arriving in the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:36.320
Peter is determined to help Doctor Strange&nbsp;
capture them and return them to their own worlds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:40.400
That all changes when Peter discovers that&nbsp;
almost all the villains he's captured died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:45.280
in their universes. Knowing that returning them&nbsp;
home means sending them to their imminent deaths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:47.600
Peter resolves to find a way to save them all.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:52.160
The simple solution for saving the villains is to&nbsp;
"cure" them of their various abilities. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:56.160
if Spider-Man's foes aren't superpowered, they&nbsp;
won't need to fight the hero to the death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:00.400
It takes some multiversal assistance&nbsp;
— and some serious sacrifice — for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:03.840
Peter to find a cure for each of&nbsp;
the five baddies he's up against.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:07.920
Ultimately, he's able to de-power everyone&nbsp;
before repairing the cracks in the universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:12.640
and sending them all home. How does that&nbsp;
impact the original universe of each villain?

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:16.800
"No Way Home" doesn't show what happens in the&nbsp;
Raimi-verse and Webb-verse when everyone returns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:20.880
but the changes are undoubtedly huge. No&nbsp;
one would remember their time in the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:25.920
but all the villains would be returned to good&nbsp;
guy status — or at least neutral guy status — well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:30.480
before they died. Because Harry Osborn didn't&nbsp;
need to seek revenge for his father's death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:34.800
is he still alive in the Raimi-verse? Without&nbsp;
the final battle against Electro taking place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:39.360
is Gwen Stacy alive in the Webb-verse?&nbsp;
All that could be on the table now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:42.080
but we'll have to wait for future&nbsp;
announcements to know for sure.

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:48.640
At the climax of "No Way Home," the multiverse&nbsp;
is collapsing, and Doctor Strange is doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:53.840
everything in his power to prevent all of reality&nbsp;
from being pulled into the confines of the MCU.

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:59.280
Clearly, whatever is coming through from alternate&nbsp;
universes would be immensely destructive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:02.800
but Strange plays coy about&nbsp;
the specifics of the danger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:07.200
"They are coming," he tells Peter, but he&nbsp;
doesn't bother to explain who "they" are.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:10.720
"They're starting to come&nbsp;
through, and I can't stop them."

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:15.520
Two possibilities seem most likely here.&nbsp;
First, Marvel's "What If?" on Disney+ has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:19.280
already shown that there's no shortage of&nbsp;
dangerous characters out in the multiverse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:23.920
On the other hand, "Eternals" recently introduced&nbsp;
a whole slew of new dangers into the MCU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:29.120
In Strange's struggle against the multiverse,&nbsp;
mysterious glowing figures are only briefly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:34.080
shown peeking through the cracks of reality, but&nbsp;
the visuals had a striking resemblance to some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:39.360
the Celestials we've already seen. Considering&nbsp;
how dangerous a single Celestial can be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:44.560
an army of them would be more than enough&nbsp;
to make Strange sweat bullets. Either way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:49.840
it seems very likely that "The Multiverse&nbsp;
of Madness" will give us further hints.

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:53.200
"Venom: Let There Be Carnage" sent&nbsp;
shockwaves through the nerd world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:57.760
with its end-credits scene. Eddie Brock and&nbsp;
Venom were transported into the MCU just in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:01.680
time to see Peter Parker's secret identity&nbsp;
revealed on the news by Mysterio and J.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:06.160
Jonah Jameson. We all wondered if this meant&nbsp;
Venom was officially part of the MCU and whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:11.040
or not he would team up with the other baddies&nbsp;
in "No Way Home" to create the Sinister Six.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:14.720
However, Eddie Brock and his favorite&nbsp;
parasite made only a brief appearance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:18.080
in the film's mid-credits scene. Eddie&nbsp;
is seen at a bar getting up to speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:22.400
on the incredible events of the MCU, but&nbsp;
then he's transported away when Doctor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:27.120
Strange closes the multiverse ... though&nbsp;
a small piece of symbiote is left behind.

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:29.600
The scene raises more questions than it answers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:35.280
All we know for now is that a small piece of&nbsp;
Venom has officially made its way into the MCU.

